==============================================================================
CALIBRATION AGAINST k = 5, WHERE THE INFINITE ANSWER IS KNOWN
==============================================================================
   k=5  C = 5/4    N*_5(C) = 13   (exact complete enumeration, this dir)
   k=5  C = 4/3    N*_5(C) = 35   (exact complete enumeration, this dir)
   k=5  C >= 4          N*_5(C) = +INFINITY  -- THEOREM: route R2 / CORE Thm 21
        'Construction A' is a 5-AP-free permutation of N with sup_v pos(v)/v = 4
        (verified here to N = 65535: max pos(v)/v = 3.999878, limsup = 4).

   two-point exponential fit for k=5:  N*_5(C) ~ 4.593e-06 exp(11.885 C)
      -> predicts a FINITE N*_5(4) = 2.03e+15, which is FALSE.
      LESSON: an exponential fit to small-C extinction data cannot detect a
      divergence at finite C*.  In the one case where C* is known (k=5, C* <= 4)
      the fit gives no warning whatsoever.  Therefore the k=4 fit
      N*(C) ~ a e^{bC} is NOT evidence that rho(N) -> infinity.
